<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian the Younger of Brunswick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1599">1599</a>–<a href="/years/1626">1626</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Christian of Brunswick · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian the Younger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> was a German Protestant commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and administrator of the prince-bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> from 1616. Champion of the exiled Elizabeth Stuart, he raised armies for the Palatine cause, but was defeated at Höchst in 1622 and Stadtlohn in 1623. He died of illness in 1626.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-the-younger-of-brunswick.jpg" alt="Christian the Younger of Brunswick" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anthonie van Ravesteyn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzog_Christian_von_Braunschweig-L%C3%BCneburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christian was born on 20 September 1599 at Gröningen Priory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, the third son of Duke Henry Julius of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>, and his second wife Elizabeth, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a>. After his father's death he was brought up by his uncle, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>, and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-helmstedt">University of Helmstedt</a>. In 1616, at 17, he was elected Lutheran administrator of Halberstadt after his brother Rudolf. Neither the emperor nor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> confirmed him, but the post gave him the money for a military career, and he became known as der tolle Halberstädter, the daredevil from Halberstadt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1620 he joined the army of Prince Maurice of Orange in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and in 1621 he raised his own army for Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, declaring a chivalric love for Frederick's wife Elizabeth. By the end of that year he had 10,000 men, wintered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and took great sums from the dioceses of Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. Beaten by Johan t'Serclaes, Count of Tilly, at Höchst on 22 June 1622, he escaped with much of his army and joined Ernst von Mansfeld, with whom he quarrelled so badly that they nearly fought a duel.</p><p>At the Battle of Fleurus on 29 August 1622 he led five cavalry charges against the Spanish under Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> before breaking through to help relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom">Bergen op Zoom</a>, at the cost of most of his infantry and one arm; he then took the motto Altera restat, the other remains. In 1623 a plan with Mansfeld and Bethlen Gabor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a>, to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> failed, and on 6 August Tilly caught him short of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border at Stadtlohn, where his army of 15,000 was reduced to 2,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Three days after Stadtlohn, Frederick V signed an armistice with Emperor Ferdinand II, closing the Palatine phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. When Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> entered the war in 1625, Christian received new funds and was ordered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, but was checked by Tilly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and withdrew. Ill from the start of the campaign, he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> on 16 June 1626, aged 26. The historian Herfried Münkler holds that he was driven less by Protestant conviction than by a love of adventure and a romantic view of himself as a knight.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian the Younger of Brunswick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years War its administrator, <b>Christian of Brunswick</b>, fought for the Protestant side.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-halberstadt">Prince-Bishopric of Halberstadt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gröningen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.93,11.22&amp;year=1616" aria-label="Gröningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 369 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christian the Younger of Brunswick"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 639h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 639h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.93,11.22&amp;year=1616">What was here in 1616? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
